Results: a header that folds, a working Home tab, and Direct that means direct

Eight things from testing the last build on a phone.

Direct now means direct. The stops filter read the outbound leg only, so
somebody who asked for a direct return flight was shown fares that changed
planes on the way home - a connection hiding in the half of the trip they
were not looking at. Both legs are checked; unknown still survives, on both,
for the reason at the top of that file. Two tests cover it.

The Home tab did nothing on the results screen. It navigated with
`saveState`, which saved the stack it had just popped, and `restoreState`,
which put it straight back - so the app returned to exactly the page
somebody was trying to leave. Standing deeper inside a tab now pops to that
tab's root instead.

Edit no longer walks people back to the front of the app. It opened the home
screen, past the results they were reading, which were then gone. It now
opens the search summary - the same four answers with an Edit beside each
and a Search at the bottom - which arrives from below like a sheet and
leaves the list on screen behind it. The word became a pencil: "Modifier"
and "تعديل" are both wider than the English, in a bar that also holds a
route and a date.

The header folds. Route, three controls, carriers and count together ate a
third of the screen on every scroll. It now folds when the list moves down
and returns when it moves up, driven by the scroll delta rather than the
position, so it answers the finger rather than where the list happens to be.

The chart button went. It and the calendar opened the same screen - the
chart is that page with a different tab selected, and the page has its own
control to switch. Two buttons into one room is a menu nobody read back.
Direct took the slot: the filter people reach for on every search, two taps
deep until now.

Airline marks sit on a white disc in both themes. Many carriers supply their
logo on its own white background rather than as a transparent PNG, so on a
dark circle each one showed as a white rectangle inside a dark ring. A white
disc is what an app icon does, and it is the only treatment that is right
for every mark we do not control.

And the list stops. A search can come back with several hundred offers, and
a list that long is not a list - it is a scroll with no end. Ten at a time,
with the number remaining on the button, because "more" alone says nothing
about how much is left and a list that ends without saying so reads as one
that failed to load the rest.

Checked on a device in both themes, which is now the rule: the fold, the
pencil, the sheet, Direct filtering 21 offers down to 3, the Home tab, and
the load-more control.
